{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/proofs/storage/Proofs.lean","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T08:27:44.592260Z","id":"27de815854a26850ceeecec524baadc372c7b53e8b6aa2b15bc807e455d57bd0","body":{"detail":"The restoration theorems (restoration_preserves_shape, restoration_commit_bounds, restoration_orders_log, restoration_exact_indices, restoration_log_representation and others, Proofs.lean:870-1407) all take a hypothesis `execution : RecoveryReturns (Jarl.state_State_restore ...) (.ok output)`. No theorem shows that restore returns .ok for any input. By contrast, append, grow, truncation and installation each have theorems proving that the operation returns. So the restore statements stay true if src/state.rs:45-75 rejects more than it should. Examples: always returning Err(Error::State); tightening `entry.id.term < last.term` to `<=`, which rejects equal-term successors (the normal case); or tightening `hard.commit > state.last().index` to `>=`. A stronger guard only makes the derived facts easier to satisfy. Such a regression would stop voters from restarting from a valid durable checkpoint. Every restore mutation in jarl/tests/proof_cases/storage.rs:37-45 weakens a guard to `false`; none strengthens one.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":870},"end":{"line":1407},"content_hash":"064fcef571d8d4d132aeb8b9e193140d5d8c8f9832d3ea2a67da2e4de7d81557"},"suggested_fix":"Add an acceptance theorem: if hard/snapshot satisfy the initial guard, the iterator yields an ordered chain from the base of length at most CAP, and base.index <= commit <= last.index, then State::restore returns .ok with LogRep. At minimum, add concrete .ok witnesses, such as an empty log and a two-entry equal-term log. Then add strengthening mutations to storage.rs that must fail with 'Lean rejected Proofs.lean'.","summary":"Every State::restore contract assumes restore succeeds, and none proves that a valid checkpoint is accepted, so a restore that rejects valid states still satisfies them","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/proofs/storage/Proofs.lean","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T10:08:39.037909Z","id":"189d6569035a4e176993473785af66b16b247bb8343f372007cc3a5f8b5b483d","body":{"detail":"storage/Proofs.lean:870-1407: restoration_preserves_shape, final_guard (:1022), places (:1041), commit_bounds (:1055), initial_guard (:1076), orders_log (:1255), exact_indices (:1327) and log_representation (:1393) all take an `execution : RecoveryReturns ... (.ok output)` hypothesis. restoration_fuel_sufficient (:924) proves only recoveryFuelSafe. state_State_restore appears in no other proof file. storage.rs:37-45 replaces each restore guard (src/state.rs:52-71) with `false`, which weakens it; none strengthens one, for example `<` to `<=`.","kind":"comment","references":"27de815854a26850ceeecec524baadc372c7b53e8b6aa2b15bc807e455d57bd0","summary":"confirmed: every restoration theorem assumes RecoveryReturns (... ) (.ok output), and none proves that a valid checkpoint is accepted","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
